course: world
lesson: 44
title: The United States and its states
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The United States of America is a huge country in North America made of 50 states; its capital is Washington, D.C., which is not in any state, and its largest city is New York City.
explain: Forty-eight states lie together between Canada and Mexico, from the Atlantic Ocean in the east to the Pacific Ocean in the west; Alaska lies far to the north-west next to Canada, and Hawaii is a chain of volcanic islands in the middle of the Pacific.
explain: The land is like a giant bowl: the old, low Appalachian Mountains in the east, the wide flat Great Plains in the middle, the high Rocky Mountains in the west through states such as Colorado, and the hot dry deserts of the Southwest in Arizona, Nevada and southern California.
explain: The Mississippi River flows south through the middle of the country into the Gulf of Mexico, and the five Great Lakes on the border with Canada hold about a fifth of all the fresh lake water on the Earth's surface.
explain: The country is so wide that it has several time zones: when it is 9 o'clock in the morning in New York, it is only 6 o'clock in Los Angeles, because the Sun rises in the east first.
fact: The United States has 50 states.
fact: The capital of the United States is Washington, D.C.
fact: New York City is the largest city in the United States.
fact: Alaska is the largest state and lies in the far north-west, next to Canada.
fact: Hawaii is a chain of volcanic islands in the Pacific Ocean.
fact: Texas is the largest of the 48 states that lie together.
fact: The Mississippi River flows south into the Gulf of Mexico.
fact: The Rocky Mountains run north to south through the western United States.
fact: The five Great Lakes lie on the border between the United States and Canada.
fact: Death Valley in California is one of the hottest places on Earth.
fact: Florida is a warm peninsula in the south-east, between the Atlantic Ocean and the Gulf of Mexico.
fact: The 48 states that lie together have four main time zones, and the East is three hours ahead of the West Coast.
fact: California, with the city of Los Angeles, is on the West Coast by the Pacific; New York and Florida are on the East Coast by the Atlantic.
exercise: How many states does the United States have? | 50 | 48 | 52 | 13
exercise: What is the capital of the United States? | Washington, D.C. | New York City | Los Angeles | Chicago
exercise: Which is the largest city in the United States? | New York City | Washington, D.C. | Houston | Miami
exercise: Which is the largest state of the United States? | Alaska | Texas | California | Hawaii
exercise: What is Hawaii? | Volcanic islands in the Pacific | A dry desert state in the Southwest | A group of islands in the Atlantic | A mountain state up next to Canada
exercise: Into which body of water does the Mississippi River flow? | The Gulf of Mexico | The Pacific Ocean in the west | Lake Michigan | The Arctic Ocean
exercise: Which mountains run north to south through the western United States? | The Rocky Mountains | The Appalachian Mountains | The Alps | The Andes
exercise: Where are the Great Lakes? | On the US border with Canada | In the deserts of the Southwest | In the middle of the state of Texas | On the biggest island of Hawaii
exercise: Which part of the United States has hot dry deserts? | The Southwest | The North-east | Alaska | Florida
exercise: Which state is a warm peninsula in the south-east? | Florida | Colorado | Alaska | Oregon
exercise: Which mountains are older and lower, in the eastern United States? | The Appalachians | The Rockies | The Himalayas | The Carpathians
exercise: What lies between the Appalachians and the Rocky Mountains? | The wide flat Great Plains | A great inland sea of salt water | The deserts of Arizona and Nevada | The volcanic Hawaiian islands
exercise: When it is noon in New York, what time is it in Los Angeles? | 9 o'clock in the morning | 3 o'clock in the afternoon | Noon as well | Midnight
exercise: Why does the United States need several time zones? | It is so wide that the Sun rises at very different times | Each state chooses any time it likes | The Sun moves much faster over the west than over the east | Clocks run slower in the mountains
exercise: Which of these is in California? | Death Valley | The Great Lakes | The Mississippi delta | Mount Washington
test: A girl in Texas flies to the capital of her country. Where does she land? | Washington, D.C. | New York City | Austin | Houston
test: A boy in Hawaii wants to visit the largest state by car. Why can he not drive there? | Hawaii is a group of islands in the middle of the Pacific | Alaska has no roads at all | The largest state is Texas, which is too far | Cars are not allowed in Hawaii
test: A paper boat is dropped into the Mississippi in Minnesota and floats all the way down. Where does it end up? | In the Gulf of Mexico | In the Great Lakes | In the Pacific Ocean | In Hudson Bay in Canada
test: A family drives from New York west to Colorado. Which do they cross before reaching the high Rockies? | The Appalachians and the Great Plains | The Pacific coast and the islands of Hawaii | The Gulf of Mexico and the Florida coast | The Great Lakes and then the whole of Alaska
test: A boy in Florida calls his aunt in California at 8 o'clock in the evening his time. What time is it for his aunt? | 5 o'clock in the afternoon | 11 o'clock at night | 8 o'clock in the evening | 8 o'clock in the morning
test: Which state would be best for seeing glaciers, moose and very long summer days? | Alaska | Florida | Arizona | Hawaii
test: Which is true about Washington, D.C.? | It is the capital and is not part of any state | It is the largest city of the country | It is in the state of Washington on the Pacific | It is in the middle of the Great Plains
test: A girl in Arizona sees cacti and very little rain all year. In which region does she live? | The desert Southwest | The Great Lakes region | The rainy Pacific North-west | The snowy North-east
test: Which list gives places from east to west across the United States? | New York, Colorado, California | California, Colorado, New York | Colorado, New York, California | New York, California, Colorado
test: it's 9 in the morning in new york, what time in LA then | 6 in the morning | 12 noon | 9 in the morning | 3 in the morning
test: which state is way up north next to canada n is the biggest | Alaska | Texas | Hawaii | Florida
test: my cousin says New York City is the capital of the USA. true? | No, the capital is Washington, D.C. | Yes, it is the biggest so it is the capital | Yes, and Washington is a state | No, the capital is Los Angeles
test: a kid in my class reckons Hawaii's next to Florida. right? | No, Hawaii is volcanic islands in the Pacific Ocean | Yes, it is beside Florida in the Atlantic | Yes, it is in the Gulf of Mexico | No, Hawaii is in the Great Lakes
exam: State the number of states constituting the United States. | Fifty | Forty-eight | Fifty-two | Thirteen
exam: Identify the capital of the United States. | Washington, D.C. | New York City | Los Angeles | Chicago
exam: Identify the largest state of the United States by area. | Alaska | Texas | California | Hawaii
exam: It is given that it is noon on the West Coast of the United States. Ascertain the hour on the East Coast. | Three o'clock in the afternoon | Nine o'clock in the morning | Noon likewise | Six o'clock in the evening
